---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/finishes/"
  title="Packaging Finishes | Foil, Soft-Touch, Spot UV"
  description="Lamination, foil stamping, spot UV, embossing and varnish for lip packaging, with cost and recyclability trade-offs."
  h1="Packaging Finishes and Coatings"
  eyebrow="Specification"
  lead="Coating does two jobs: it protects print from handling and it changes how the pack is judged before it is opened. Each option carries a cost and, in most cases, a disposal consequence."
  ogTitle="Finishes and Coatings for Cosmetic Cartons"
  ogDescription="Soft-touch, matte and gloss lamination, foil, spot UV, emboss and varnish compared for lip packaging."
  points={['Protection and appearance', 'Cost per pass', 'Honest disposal notes']}
  ctaHeading="Want to see a finish before committing?"
  ctaBody="We can supply material swatches and a production-standard sample with your foil and spot UV in place, so you approve something you have held rather than something on a screen."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="protection-first"
    heading="Start with how the pack is handled"
    intro="Appearance tends to dominate this conversation, but on lip packaging durability is the more practical consideration."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A lip balm carton on a peg fixture is picked up and put back repeatedly by
      shoppers. A lipstick box sits in a drawer for months. A gloss carton
      travels inside a mailer. Those three products need different amounts of
      surface protection, and paying for a finish the pack does not need is as
      wasteful as skipping one it does.
    </p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        <strong>Self-service retail</strong> — laminate or a hard varnish, because
        bare digital print scuffs at the seams within weeks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Boxed e-commerce</strong> — a varnish is often enough, since the
        carton is protected by its mailer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gift and limited edition</strong> — the finish is doing brand work
        as much as protection, and the budget shifts accordingly.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="lamination"
    heading="Laminates and varnishes"
    intro="These cover the whole surface. Everything else on this page is applied selectively on top."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Full-surface coatings compared."
      columns={['Coating', 'Look and feel', 'Durability', 'Disposal']}
      rows={[
        [
          'Matte lamination',
          'Soft, non-reflective, understated',
          'High, though finger marks show on dark solids',
          'Plastic film; consumer must separate it',
        ],
        [
          'Gloss lamination',
          'Deep saturated colour, reflective',
          'Highest; shrugs off scuffing',
          'Plastic film; consumer must separate it',
        ],
        [
          'Soft-touch lamination',
          'Velvety, warm to the hand, reads premium',
          'High, marks on very dark solids',
          'Plastic film; consumer must separate it',
        ],
        [
          'Matte or satin varnish',
          'Close to the bare board, slight sheen',
          'Moderate',
          'No practical effect on paper recycling',
        ],
        [
          'Gloss varnish',
          'Shiny without a film layer',
          'Moderate to good',
          'No practical effect on paper recycling',
        ],
        [
          'Aqueous coating',
          'Neutral, minimal change to the board',
          'Moderate',
          'No practical effect on paper recycling',
        ],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="note">
      If recyclability matters to your brand, a varnish rather than a laminate is
      the single most useful substitution available. It keeps the carton in one
      material stream at some cost to durability and depth of colour.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="selective"
    heading="Selective finishes"
    intro="Applied to chosen areas of the artwork, each as its own production pass and its own artwork layer."
  >
    <ul class="info-cards">
      <li>
        <h3>Hot foil stamping</h3>
        <p>
          Real metal leaf pressed into the board with heat and pressure. Genuinely
          reflective in a way metallic ink is not. Most effective on logos and
          thin frames; large solid foil areas are harder to lay down evenly.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Cold foil</h3>
        <p>
          Applied inline during printing, so you can print over it in the same
          pass. On long runs with substantial metallic areas it can cost less than
          hot stamping and allows coloured metallics.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Spot UV</h3>
        <p>
          Gloss varnish on selected artwork over a matte background. High contrast
          for a fraction of the cost of foil, and the most economical way to
          suggest shine on a gloss carton.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Embossing and debossing</h3>
        <p>
          Raises or recesses a shape into the board. Needs no ink at all, works on
          coated and uncoated stock, and holds far more definition on rigid
          greyboard than on thin folding board.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Metallic and pearlescent inks</h3>
        <p>
          A shimmer across large areas that foil cannot economically cover.
          Subtler than foil and considerably cheaper at scale, and it keeps the
          carton in the paper stream.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Screen-printed opaque white</h3>
        <p>
          A thick opaque layer for dark or reflective stock, where offset white
          would look thin. Also used as the underprint that lets normal colours
          read on kraft or holographic board.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="combinations"
    heading="Combinations that work"
    intro="Finishes are rarely used alone. These pairings come up repeatedly because they solve a specific problem."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Common finishing combinations and what each achieves."
      columns={['Combination', 'Effect', 'Suits']}
      rows={[
        ['Matte laminate + hot foil logo', 'Foil reads as genuinely metallic against a calm background', 'Premium lipstick and gift packaging'],
        ['Matte laminate + spot UV', 'Contrast between matte and gloss without foil cost', 'Gloss cartons, mid-market ranges'],
        ['Soft-touch + emboss', 'Tactile on two levels; the pack is judged before it is read', 'Luxury and limited edition'],
        ['Gloss laminate on holographic board', 'Amplifies the iridescent shift', 'Holographic gloss packaging'],
        ['Matte laminate on holographic board', 'Diffuses the rainbow into a soft pearl shimmer', 'Pastel and quieter iridescent ranges'],
        ['Varnish only on kraft', 'Keeps the uncoated look and one material stream', 'Natural and low-waste ranges'],
        ['Black-through board + foil', 'No white cut edges anywhere, metallic detail on top', 'Dark premium cartons'],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="measure">
      The pairing worth understanding properly is laminate over reflective stock,
      because matte and gloss produce genuinely different products from the same
      sheet. That difference is set out on the
      <a href="/holographic-lip-gloss-boxes/">iridescent gloss carton page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="cost"
    heading="What each finish costs you"
    intro="Finishing costs split into tooling, which happens once, and a press or converting pass, which happens per unit."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        <strong>Varnish</strong> — inline with printing, so the cheapest option by
        a clear margin.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lamination</strong> — a converting pass with a material cost.
        Moderate, and soft-touch costs more than matte or gloss.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spot UV</strong> — a screen or plate plus a pass. Modest tooling,
        modest per-unit cost.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Foil and emboss</strong> — a metal die plus a pass. Tooling makes
        these disproportionately expensive at low volumes and quite reasonable at
        high ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Holographic or metallised board</strong> — a material premium on
        every unit that does not fall away with volume the way tooling does.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Each additional pass also adds production time. If you are working to a
      fixed launch date, tell us at quoting stage so the schedule accounts for the
      finishing rather than being revised after it is specified. Current bands are
      on the <a href="/turnaround-time/">lead time page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="artwork"
    heading="How finishes have to reach us in the artwork"
    intro="Foil, spot UV, emboss and opaque white are not colours. Each is a separate tool made from a separate layer."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      Supply each as its own vector layer drawn in 100% black and named for what
      it is: "foil gold", "spot UV gloss", "emboss", "opaque white". Where two
      overlap, say which sits on top. An unnamed extra layer gets interpreted
      rather than followed, and that costs a proof cycle. Full file requirements
      are on the <a href="/artwork-guidelines/">artwork page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
